Wang2026
========

Sensory-guided joint-learning MI-BCI study from Wang et al. (2026).

Dataset Overview
----------------
  Code: Wang2026
  Paradigm: imagery
  DOI: 10.1184/R1/32293995.v1
  Subjects: 39
  Sessions per subject: 5
  Events: left_hand=1, right_hand=2, hands=3, rest=4
  Trial interval: [0.0, 4.999] s
  File format: MATLAB v7.3
  Data preprocessed: True
  Contributing labs: Carnegie Mellon University Biomedical Functional Imaging and Neuroengineering Lab

Acquisition
-----------
  Sampling rate: 1000.0 Hz
  Number of channels: 62
  Channel types: eeg=62
  Channel names: Fp1, Fpz, Fp2, AF3, AF4, F7, F5, F3, F1, Fz, F2, F4, F6, F8, FT7, FC5, FC3, FC1, FCz, FC2, FC4, FC6, FT8, T7, C5, C3, C1, Cz, C2, C4, C6, T8, TP7, CP5, CP3, CP1, CPz, CP2, CP4, CP6, TP8, P7, P5, P3, P1, Pz, P2, P4, P6, P8, PO7, PO5, PO3, POz, PO4, PO6, PO8, CB1, O1, Oz, O2, CB2
  Montage: standard_1005
  Hardware: Compumedics Neuroscan SynAmps 2/RT
  Software: BCI2000
  Reference: midway between Cz and CPz
  Line frequency: 60.0 Hz
  Online filters: {'notch': 60.0}
  Impedance threshold: 5.0 kOhm
  Cap manufacturer: Compumedics Neuroscan
  Cap model: Quik-Cap

Ethics
------
  Every experiment involving human participants has been carried out following a protocol approved by the Institutional Review Board of Carnegie Mellon University. Each participant gave informed written consent. (Quoted verbatim from the primary publication's Ethics statement: Wang, H., Zhang, Y., Karrenbach, M., Ding, Y., & He, B. (2026). Sensory-guided human-machine joint learning accelerates the acquisition of motor imagery brain computer interface control. Nature Communications, 17, 6177. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-75435-5. Participants were recruited from the Pittsburgh area and compensated $15/hour; informed consent followed the World Medical Association's Declaration of Helsinki.)

Participants
------------
  Number of subjects: 39
  Health status: healthy
  BCI experience: naive

Experimental Protocol
---------------------
  Paradigm: imagery
  Task type: online 1D and 2D cursor control
  Number of classes: 4
  Class labels: left_hand, right_hand, hands, rest
  Trial duration: 5.0 s
  Tasks: left hand imagery, right hand imagery, bilateral hand imagery, rest
  Study design: One longitudinal MI-BCI study release with 39 unique participants. The primary randomized experiment included 31 participants: joint learning (n=15), BCI2000 control (n=8), and tactile control (n=8). A separately recruited EEGNet-control cohort added 8 participants.
  Feedback type: visual cursor feedback, with cohort-specific tactile guidance, EEGNet adaptation, or fixed BCI2000 decoding
  Stimulus type: visual cursor and directional cue
  Stimulus modalities: visual, tactile
  Primary modality: visual
  Synchronicity: synchronous
  Mode: online
  Training/test split: True
  Stimulus presentation: SoftwareName=BCI2000

